## By the Numbers
Here's a startling figure: 90 Class I recalls in 2026 so far. Class I recalls indicate a serious risk of health consequences -- think Salmonella, E. coli, or undeclared allergens leading to severe reactions. Retailers and suppliers have a long way to go in ensuring their products are safe.

## Recent Offenders
Yesterday, we saw a range of recalls from undeclared colors in 'Bombay Kitchen' snacks by Ethnic Foods Inc. to lead contamination in spice powders from Summit Import Corporation. Things go from bad to worse with Unistel Industries Inc., recalling ground allspice for Salmonella contamination. That's a clear example of negligence that warrants scrutiny.

## Monthly Trends
April 2026 closed with 84 recalls, a slight dip from March's 102 but still concerning. Allergen mislabeling and Salmonella were notable culprits, alongside foreign objects and Listeria.

## The Hall of Shame
Top brands for recalls are familiar faces: Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC lead the pack with 11 recalls each this year, both failing due to alarming Class I infractions. 'Diva Fam Inc' racks up 14 total recalls, failing to address persistent issues.

## Conclusion
Food recalls are becoming more prolific and varied. Consumers should prioritize transparency and certifications when buying food products and hold brands accountable for safety.

